  "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the limit as\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}{2x-{x}^{3}}={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}{{x}^{3}-2x}.\n$$\n\nThe numerator here is a bounded, oscillating function with\n\n$$\n|\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x|\\le 1.\n$$\n\nHowever, the denominator ${x}^{3}-2x$ grows without bound as $x\\to \\infty .$ So the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}{2x-{x}^{3}}={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}{{x}^{3}-2x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see this from the graph of $y=\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}{2x-{x}^{3}},$ shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029498/assets/a9d96a5a85181cddc6eb1bd5f79d6a0238ef16f060ddebf2dc4c48ef1911604f.png)",
